        {
          "subsection_id": "pediatric_emergency_dosing_tape_chart_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pediatric_emergency_dosing_tape_chart_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is this instrument?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is a pediatric emergency dosing tape or chart, used to estimate a child’s weight from length and guide emergency medication dosing or equipment selection."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the principle behind its use?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It uses the relationship between a child’s length and expected body weight to provide a rapid estimate, often linked to color-coded recommendations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Does it measure actual weight?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It estimates weight. An actual measured weight should be used when available and appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the most important precaution before administering a medication?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Verify the drug, dose, concentration, route, calculated volume, maximum dose and applicable patient-specific restrictions against an approved protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Can it be used for every newborn?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Neonatal dosing may require measured weight, gestational age and postnatal age. A standard pediatric length-based tape should not automatically be used for neonatal dosing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What are its limitations?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may be inaccurate in children with obesity, severe malnutrition or unusual body proportions. Incorrect measurement and outdated or mismatched charts can also cause errors."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Should measurement delay treatment in a critically ill child?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Begin indicated emergency care immediately and use the appropriate approved protocol. Do not delay lifesaving interventions to obtain a length measurement."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pediatric_emergency_dosing_tape_chart_subsection_17",
          "subsection_title": "Common mistakes and examiner tips",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pediatric_emergency_dosing_tape_chart_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Common mistakes"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Calling the tape a weighing scale."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Measuring from the wrong anatomical landmarks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reading the wrong color zone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Using a dosing chart that does not match the tape."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confusing mg with mL."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ignoring the drug’s maximum recommended dose."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assuming an estimated weight is always accurate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Delaying emergency care while trying to obtain a measurement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Examiner tips"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "State that the tape estimates weight rather than measuring it."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Demonstrate the correct positioning and measurement technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain how you would find the corresponding chart."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Emphasize medication verification and independent double-checks where required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mention the limitations in children with abnormal body habitus."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain that neonatal dosing may require a different approach."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "pediatric_emergency_dosing_tape_chart_subsection_18",
          "subsection_title": "Mini-OSCE practice station",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "pediatric_emergency_dosing_tape_chart_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Practice station",
                    "Scenario"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "You are given a pediatric emergency dosing tape and its accompanying chart. A child presents to the emergency department with severe respiratory distress. The examiner asks you to demonstrate how you would use the tape safely."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Your tasks"
                  ]
                },
                {
